The '''Great Catholic Schism''' was the division of the Catholic Church in Illypnia in 1313 between the papal states, centred on [[Volisania]], and a rival Zamarran court established at [[Zamarra]] on [[Lake Zamarra]]. The schism arose from disputes over church authority, taxation, indulgences, purgatory, and Curial administration. It contributed to the [[Two Centuries War]], which began in 1337.

== Background ==

By the early 14th century, ecclesiastical authority in much of Illypnia was concentrated in the papal states around Volisania. Several kingdoms to the south and east regarded papal taxation and jurisdiction as burdensome, especially those with limited access to the main church revenues and trade routes. In [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]], a landlocked kingdom on the southern side of the Sierra Madre, criticism of indulgences, purgatory, and Curial corruption gained support at court.

Economic conditions also shaped the dispute. The [[Jade Sea War]] in eastern Fosia reduced revenue from the southern trade routes that linked Illypnia to the wider region. This reduced customs and transit income for kingdoms such as Kastruneto and [[Kingdom of Breisland|Breisland]]. Papal demands continued to flow through the church hierarchy, and for these kingdoms papal authority increasingly appeared costly.

== Outbreak ==

After the death of the sitting pope, the conclave elected the Kastrunetean [[Augustin V]]. He refused to relocate the papal court to the papal states and instead established his court at Zamarra on Lake Zamarra. The decision created a rival centre of Catholic authority and formalised the schism.

The Zamarran court was recognised by [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]], [[Kingdom of Breisland|Breisland]], and [[Kingdom of Nabia|Nabia]]. The papal states retained support from [[Volisania]], [[Sarta]], and [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]]. The division reflected doctrinal disputes as well as fiscal systems, trade interests, and diplomatic alignments.

== The Two Centuries War ==

The schism escalated into the [[Two Centuries War]], which began in 1337. The earliest and most sustained fighting took place in the valleys of the [[Rio Grande]] and [[Rio Verde]], especially in the border zone between [[Sarta]] and [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]]. Control of river corridors, valley fortresses, passes, and revenue routes was central to the conflict.

The war was fought over several generations. It consisted of frontier offensives, raids, truces, and regional campaigns rather than a single continuous war. In its earlier phases, it retained many features associated with high medieval warfare, including armoured cavalry, noble retinues, ransoms, and siege operations.

== Effects in Sarta ==

In [[Sarta]], the schism reinforced existing north-south divisions. The northern parts of the kingdom remained more closely tied to Petran Catholic authority, while the south had stronger ecclesiastical and trade connections to [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]] and the Zamarran sphere. Sarta's rulers aligned with the papal states, in part because of those northern church ties and in part because of their broader regional alliances.

The schism did not create Sarta's internal divisions, but it strengthened them and placed the kingdom within a wider confessional struggle.

